    {
      "id": "PRP-02",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "A uniform exception bound from zero density and mean square",
      "anchor": "inputs-prime-bad",
      "statement": "For suitable prime progression P in (√U,U] with |P|≥U^(9/10) and ≤R primitive base characters of conductor ≤F≤√U, the primes failing at some twist or height in [Y,Y^K] occupy ≤η|P| past a common threshold.",
      "hypotheses": "General stage: 0<ε≤1/4, τ>0, K≥1, η>0, positive modulus divisible by 8, primitive reduced class, log-modulus and upper and lower height limits of admitted; each prime divisor of the modulus is ≤Y^(ε/5) or >(Y^K)^400.",
      "definitions": [
        "primitive twist conductors",
        "prime-dependent bad height",
        "partial derivative of a clipped weight",
        "dyadic intervals",
        "mean square",
        "relative prime count"
      ],
      "equations": [
        "inputs-prime-density"
      ],
      "dependencies": [
        "APC-01"
      ],
      "correspondence": "A prime failure injects into a bad primitive character since fp=f′p′ and f′≤F<p forces p=p′. The ratio of the zero-density exception count to the population is O(U^(−21/40)). For a witness height depending on p, control the derivative by the same quadratic mean square with coefficients restricted to n>y. Cauchy–Schwarz and integration give 4CV log(2V)|P| on each dyadic block. Markov and summation give (32C/τ)Y^(−τ)/(1−2^(−τ)). Sum the latter over ≤R characters with allocation η/(2(R+1)); add the η/2 zero-density allowance. Outside both sets the explicit formula estimates the centered sum.",
