      "id": "PRP-03",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "Choosing both primes and obtaining the coefficient four",
      "anchor": "inputs-prime-choice",
      "statement": "Both prime progressions have surviving proportion at least 1/2. The first controls the q twist. The second controls the d and qd twists for every surviving q with a threshold independent of q. Final outputs are the centered sum bound y^(1/2+ε/2), derivative bound 4log y and the height-specific zero-free box.",
      "hypotheses": "First N₀=8M. For stage two use primitivizations of ξ(·)(·/q)^i for i=0,1 with ≤2#characters(N₀) choices and conductor ≤N₀ q. For the rectangle use real primitive ρ with ρ.N|8M. For the analytic estimates use all base characters modulo M and primitive inducing characters, with (i,j) nonzero.",
      "definitions": [
        "uniform family size",
        "conditional selection of the second prime",
        "logarithmic derivative at one",
        "stage-two modulus admissibility"
      ],
      "equations": [],
      "dependencies": [
        "PRP-01",
        "PRP-02"
      ],
      "correspondence": "With τ=ε/2 and η=1/2, form both thresholds before the first prime choice. The first and second conductor budgets are 8M and 8M exp z respectively. Change level at base characters and distinguish the j=0 and j=1 cases. Coefficient 4 follows from the log-log estimate and 1000ε/5≤50/13<4, with lower order terms absorbed by enlarging Y.",
